#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:1
msgid "Writing a methodology"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:3
msgid ""
"A methodology is a page of prose. Nothing about it is executable, nothing"
" about it is checked by a program, and the tool that uses it never reads "
"a word of it except to find one optional section."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:6
msgid ""
"That is not a limitation to work around. It is the whole idea, and this "
"chapter is about writing one well."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:9
msgid "What a methodology is for"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:11
msgid ""
"When somebody grants a file, what they sign is roughly: *I applied the "
"protocol described in this text to this file, and I stand behind the "
"result.* The methodology is that text. So the question a methodology page"
" has to answer is not \"what should the tool check?\" — the tool checks "
"hashes and signatures and nothing else — but:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:16
msgid ""
"**What is the person who grants a file under this methodology claiming, "
"and what are they not claiming?**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:19
msgid ""
"A reader downstream will one day look at a `.csv` in a formats "
"repository, see that three people vouched for it under *basic check*, and"
" decide how much that is worth. What decides that is your page. If it "
"says something a careful person can honestly do and honestly report, the "
"grant means something. If it is a checklist nobody could truthfully "
"complete, the grant is theatre, and the mechanism around it — hashes, "
"signatures, sources — is theatre too."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:25
msgid "The one test that matters"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:28
msgid ""
"Read your protocol steps and ask: *could a competent, honest person do "
"exactly this, in an afternoon, and know whether they had done it?* A step"
" that cannot be honestly completed does not raise the trust level of the "
"methodology. It lowers it, because the grants made under it were made by "
"people who ticked it anyway."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:34
msgid ""
"Two consequences follow, and both are worth saying out loud on the page "
"itself:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:36
msgid ""
"**A methodology is not a quality gate.** It does not say the data is "
"right. It says what a person did and what they concluded. `basic check` "
"says somebody ran the thing and looked at the output; `golden standard` "
"says somebody checked every value against the source document. Neither "
"says the extraction is correct, because no human procedure says that."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:41
msgid ""
"**Weak methodologies are useful.** The temptation, writing a first "
"methodology, is to make it demanding so that the grant sounds impressive."
" Resist it. A weak claim that is true is worth more than a strong claim "
"that is aspirational, and a repository is far better served by a hundred "
"honest `basic check` grants than by three `golden standard` grants nobody"
" could really have earned."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:46
msgid "The anatomy of a page"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:48
msgid ""
"The three published methodologies share a shape, and a new one should "
"follow it. Only the last row is read by a program; every other section "
"exists for the person deciding whether to grant, and for the person "
"downstream deciding what the grant is worth."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:25
msgid "Section"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:25
msgid "Answers"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:25
msgid "**Purpose**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:25
msgid "in one or two sentences, what a grant under this methodology asserts"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:25
msgid "**Scope**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:25
msgid "what kinds of thing it applies to, in prose"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:25
msgid "**Protocol steps**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:25
msgid "what the granter actually does, in order, concretely enough to follow"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:25
msgid "**Trust level**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:25
msgid "how strong the resulting claim is, said plainly and without flattering it"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:25
msgid "**Applicable context**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:25
msgid "when to reach for this one rather than another"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:25
msgid "**Limitations**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:25
msgid "what a grant under it does **not** establish"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:25
msgid "**Supported paths**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:25
msgid ""
"*optional, and machine-read* — the repository paths it covers, each with "
"a gloss"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:62
msgid ""
"The order is a reading order, not a rule. What matters is that "
"**Limitations** is there and is honest: it is the section a downstream "
"reader trusts you for, and a page without one reads like marketing."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:66
msgid ""
"Here is the skeleton, in reStructuredText, which is the format a "
"methodology page is written in:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:96
msgid "Declaring the paths it covers"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:98
msgid ""
"Everything above is prose for people. `Supported paths` is the one "
"section a program reads — and even there, what it reads is only the "
"patterns; the prose under each of them is shown to the reader untouched, "
"never interpreted."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:119
msgid ""
"It is an ordinary, **visible** section, and each entry is a definition-"
"list term that is a single inline literal with an indented gloss under "
"it. Anything else in the section is prose and is ignored. Two deliberate "
"non-choices are worth knowing: it is not a hidden comment block, because "
"that would put the declaration where a reader of the published page could"
" not see it; and it is not a custom Sphinx directive, because that would "
"break every plain-reStructuredText consumer — including the tool itself, "
"which fetches the page as text from wherever you published it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:126
msgid "The grammar"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:128
msgid "Four tokens, and everything else is literal:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:25
msgid "Token"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:25
msgid "Matches"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:25
msgid "`*`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:25
msgid "any part of **one** path segment; never crosses a `/`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:25
msgid "`**`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:25
msgid "zero or more whole segments"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:25
msgid "a trailing `/`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:25
msgid "the directory and everything under it — the same as appending `**`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:25
msgid "anything else"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:25
msgid "itself"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:137
msgid ""
"No character classes, no braces, no negation, no `?`. Patterns are always"
" relative to the repository root, and a leading `/` is ignored rather "
"than refused."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:140
msgid ""
"The grammar is this small on purpose. These patterns are read by people "
"deciding whether to trust a grant, and a pattern language that *looked* "
"like a shell glob without being one would be worse than having none: the "
"reader would believe something the tool does not do."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:144
msgid ""
"`**` is not decoration, and the commonest mistake is reaching for `*` "
"where the real tree needs it. A formats repository may nest a **variant**"
" between the format and its outputs, and may equally not — both layouts "
"are ordinary, and a real repository contains a mixture:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:155
msgid ""
"`*` matches exactly one segment, so `tests/formats/*/out/*.csv` covers "
"the first and misses the second, and `tests/formats/*/*/out/*.csv` does "
"precisely the reverse. Neither is what you meant. "
"`tests/formats/**/out/*.csv` covers both, because `**` is *zero or more* "
"segments — which is the whole reason the grammar has a token for that and"
" not merely for \"one\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:160
msgid ""
"Check yours against real paths before publishing, from the repository the"
" patterns are about:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:169
msgid ""
"It prints nothing and answers in its exit status, like `test`. To see "
"everything a pattern would cover in a repository as it stands — which is "
"also the denominator of its coverage figure:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:176
msgid "What follows from declaring, and from not declaring"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:178
msgid ""
"**A page with no `Supported paths` section supports any path.** That is "
"the behaviour from before the section existed, and it is the honest "
"answer for a methodology whose scope genuinely cannot be written as a set"
" of paths. Leaving the section out is a legitimate choice, not an "
"omission."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:182
msgid ""
"A section that exists and declares *nothing* is read the other way — as a"
" page whose author opened the subject in order to constrain it and then "
"named none — and `grant` says so, because that is almost certainly a "
"mistake in the page rather than a methodology that covers nothing."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:186
msgid ""
"Where a grant names a path outside the declared set, the three moments "
"behave differently on purpose:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:25
msgid "Moment"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:25
msgid "What happens"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:25
msgid "`grant`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:25
msgid ""
"**refused**, printing every pattern with its gloss. Overridable with "
"`--force`, or by answering the question it asks at a terminal"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:25
msgid "`check-grants`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:25
msgid "a **warning**, never a failure"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:25
msgid "the three lookups"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:25
msgid "listed, and marked `(!)`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:195
msgid ""
"Refusing at grant time is cheap and catches the mistake while its author "
"is standing there. Failing at check time would break somebody's "
"repository because *you* edited your page, which is not a power a "
"methodology author should have."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:199
msgid "The same path in two repositories"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:201
msgid ""
"`tests/formats/` exists in a formats repository and in the engine's own "
"repository, and it means different things in the two. A pattern cannot "
"tell them apart, and the tool does not try."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:204
msgid ""
"**Resolve it in the gloss.** This is the reason the prose under each "
"pattern is required reading rather than decoration:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:215
msgid ""
"A reader who is about to grant sees that sentence in the refusal message "
"if their path does not match, and sees it on the published page if it "
"does. That is where the ambiguity gets resolved: by a person having "
"written down which one they meant."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:219
msgid "A worked example: a methodology for assertions"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:221
msgid ""
"The three published methodologies do not all look alike, and the "
"interesting one is **`agreement and good faith`**, because what it grants"
" is not a test output at all."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:224
msgid ""
"Its subject is an **assertion**: a statement in the documentation. "
"Something like *\"the extraction algorithm does not silently drop rows it"
" cannot parse\"* — a claim about the software, written as prose, which "
"somebody can read, understand and agree with. Granting it does not mean "
"you ran anything. It means you read the statement, you understood it "
"within your own technical competence, and you are willing to put your "
"name to agreeing with it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:230
msgid "Read its trust level as its author wrote it:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:232
msgid ""
"This methodology provides **personal certification** — it represents an "
"individual's good-faith agreement based on their current technical "
"understanding, but does not guarantee objective truth or comprehensive "
"verification."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:236
msgid ""
"That sentence is the whole methodology in miniature, and it is a model of"
" the kind of honesty the section asks for. It does not say the assertion "
"is true. It says a named person, with stated limitations, agreed with it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:240
msgid ""
"Its `Supported paths` therefore look nothing like the other two. There "
"are no test outputs to name:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:253
msgid "Three things in that gloss are worth copying:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:255
msgid ""
"it says which repository the path is in, because the same path elsewhere "
"is not the same thing;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:256
msgid ""
"it says what the grant is a claim **about** — the statement, not an "
"output — which is the distinction that makes this methodology different "
"from the other two;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:258
msgid ""
"it says what it does **not** reach, so a reader who came looking for "
"their own assertions is told why they are not covered rather than left to"
" guess."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:261
msgid ""
"Compare with `basic check`, whose patterns name test outputs and whose "
"gloss talks about a program having run. Same section, same grammar, and "
"two completely different kinds of claim — which is exactly what a "
"methodology is for."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:265
msgid "Pinning what the page cites"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:267
msgid ""
"A methodology page is prose, and prose cites things: a diagram, a "
"specification, another document. A grant made under the page is in part a"
" claim about what those things said. So the page can pin each of them by "
"hash, in an ordinary reStructuredText comment:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:281
msgid ""
"`..` followed by text that is not a directive is a comment in every "
"reStructuredText parser: it renders as nothing, breaks no build, and "
"reads plainly in the source. The target is either an absolute URI or a "
"path relative to the page itself."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:285
msgid ""
"**These are not a second thing to trust.** They are lines of the page, so"
" the page's own `sha256` — the one every validation document records — "
"already commits to every one of them. Following them is a deepening, and "
"it happens by default:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:294
msgid ""
"You do not write those lines by hand. Let the tool fetch and hash "
"everything the page cites:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:300
msgid ""
"It is idempotent, it refuses to touch a page it did not fetch from your "
"own filesystem, and when it does write it tells you loudly that the "
"page's hash has changed — which is the next section."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:303
msgid "Publishing it, and what publishing commits you to"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:305
msgid ""
"A methodology becomes usable the moment somebody's `validate.sources` can"
" resolve it. A source is a pattern with exactly one `*`, standing for the"
" methodology's relative name:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:315
msgid ""
"The name `my methodology` is looked for at `methodologies/my_methodology`"
" under each source in turn, so a published tree looks like this:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:324
msgid ""
"Order is priority, and the arrangement above is the common one: the "
"published documentation for the methodologies everybody shares, plus your"
" own for the ones you wrote. `freeports-validate sources` shows what each"
" name resolves to and warns where one source shadows another."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:328
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"{doc}`../../reference/cli/index` has the mechanics — the grammar in full,"
" the GitHub rewrite, the cache, offline behaviour. What belongs here is "
"the obligation that publishing creates:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:332
msgid ""
"**Editing a published methodology invalidates every grant made under it —"
" everywhere.**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:334
msgid ""
"A validation document records the `sha256` of the text its grants were "
"made under. Change one character of the page and that hash changes, so "
"every document that adopted it now points at a text that no longer "
"exists, in every repository you have never heard of. `check-grants` "
"reports it, and the only honest remedies are `update methodology` — which"
" **drops** every file granted under it, because those claims were about "
"the old text — or reverting your edit."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:340
msgid ""
"That is the mechanism working, not breaking. But it means a published "
"methodology is a commitment, and the time to get the wording right is "
"before anybody adopts it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:344
msgid "Two practical consequences:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:346
msgid ""
"**Version by name, not by edit.** If a methodology needs to change "
"substantively, publish `my_methodology_2` beside the first and let people"
" adopt it deliberately. Editing in place asks everyone who trusted you to"
" re-do work they already did."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:350
msgid ""
"**Typos are not free.** There is no such thing as a cosmetic change to a "
"hashed artefact. Proof-read before publishing, and expect to fix mistakes"
" by publishing a new name rather than by correcting the old one."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:354
msgid "A checklist"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:356
msgid ""
"Write **Purpose**, **Scope**, **Protocol steps**, **Trust level**, "
"**Applicable context** and **Limitations**, in prose, for a person."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:358
msgid ""
"Read the protocol steps back and ask whether an honest person could "
"complete them and know they had."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:360
msgid ""
"Decide whether the scope is a set of paths. If it is not, leave "
"`Supported paths` out."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:361
msgid ""
"If it is, write each pattern with a gloss that says which repository it "
"means and what vouching for such a file claims."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:363
msgid "Check every pattern against a real path with `pathmatch.py match`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:364
msgid "Run `refresh-links` if the page cites anything."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:365
msgid "Publish it, and point a source at it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:366
msgid ""
"Check it resolves, before telling anyone: `freeports-validate check-"
"methodology \"my methodology\"`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/grants/writing-a-methodology.md:367
msgid "Treat it as frozen from the first grant made under it."
msgstr ""

